---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { REVIEWER, AUTHOR, SITE } from '../consts';
const trail = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'About', href: '/about/' }];
---
<Page
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  eyebrow="Who we are"
  description="Going Eagle is a golf improvement and equipment site built around measurement rather than opinion. Here is what we do, who does it, and why."
  intro="There is no shortage of golf advice. There is a serious shortage of golf advice that tells you which part of your game to work on first."
  trail={trail}
>
  <h2>The problem we are trying to solve</h2>
  <p>
    Ask the internet how to lower your scores and you will get a thousand tips, all technically
    correct, none of them prioritised. Work on your grip. Widen your stance. Shorten your
    backswing. Buy this driver. Every one of those might help somebody, and almost none of them
    are aimed at you specifically.
  </p>
  <p>
    Meanwhile the actual answer for most amateur golfers is unglamorous and measurable: you are
    losing more strokes than you think inside 100 yards, you are hitting driver on holes where it
    cannot help you, and the equipment you are about to buy will not fix either problem.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do differently</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>We start with the number, not the tip.</strong> Our <a href="/tools/">free tools</a>
      exist so you can find out what your round is actually losing before anyone tells you how
      to swing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One page per question.</strong> We do not publish four near-identical articles
      about fixing a slice to catch four keyword variations. There is one page, and it is the
      best one we can make.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We say what we do not know.</strong> Where we have not tested something, the page
      says so. Where a statistic does not exist, we do not invent one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We will tell you not to buy something.</strong> Often the honest recommendation is
      a lesson, or the clubs already in your bag.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    Articles are researched and written by the <strong>{AUTHOR.name}</strong>. Golf content is
    then reviewed by <a href={REVIEWER.url}>{REVIEWER.name}</a>, a golf coach with six years of
    coaching experience, who checks the advice for accuracy and practical usefulness and approves
    it before publication.
  </p>
  <p>
    We keep those roles clearly labelled, because “written by an expert” and “checked by an
    expert” are different claims and readers deserve to know which one they are getting. The
    full process is on our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>How we are funded</h2>
  <p>
    Affiliate commission, disclosed on every page that carries a link. Commission never changes
    the order of a recommendation — the reasoning is set out in
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">our disclosure</a> and
    <a href="/gear/how-we-test/">our testing methodology</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/tools/ball-flight-diagnostic/">Work out what your miss actually is</a></li>
    <li><a href="/scoring/">Find the roadmap for your scoring milestone</a></li>
    <li><a href="/gear/">Read a buying guide before you spend anything</a></li>
  </ul>
</Page>
